    <div><div lang="EN">
        <p class="legacy-fb5ec070f2">&nbsp;</p>
        <p class="legacy-fb5ec070f2">&nbsp;</p>
        <p class="legacy-fb5ec070f2"><span class="legacy-63a7ce21a5"><span class="legacy-63a7ce21a5"><span class="legacy-63a7ce21a5">1950 Wildcat Cartridge Found</span></span></span></p>
        <p class="legacy-fb5ec070f2"></p>
        <div class="legacy-8f05af8d3a">
          <p> </p>
        </div>
        <p><span class="legacy-8f05af8d3a"></span><span class="legacy-8f05af8d3a">&nbsp;
            Back in the late 1970s I received a phone call from a friend
            who needed a hand removing some junk from his basement
            garage.
          </span></p>
        <div class="legacy-8f05af8d3a">
          <p>&nbsp;
            While loading the junk on his pickup to be hauled to the
            dump I came across an old shoebox full of cartridges. He
            noticed me looking at the contents of the box over and said,
            There just a bunch of old shells. If you want them you can
            have them. That box was here in the garage when I bought the
            house years ago. I put the box in my car and took them home.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            Later that evening I took a closer look at the contents of
            the box. There was the expected old paper shot shells, 30-30
            Winchesters, 38 Specials, and so on. Nothing out of the
            ordinary, except a matchbox stuffed with odd-looking grungy
            cartridges. I rubbed the base of one to check the headstamp
            in an attempt to identify the cartridge. Much to my surprise
            the headstamp read REM-UMC 300 MAGNUM. I checked another
            and it had a headstamp of Super X 300 H&amp;H MAG. These
            were belted magnum cases, but they looked nothing like a 300
            H&amp;H Magnum. They were much shorter than a 300 H&amp;H,
            and the neck was not that of a 30 caliber. They were set
            aside to investigate sometime in the future.</p>
          <p>&nbsp;Funny how fast thirty some years can fly by when one
            is busy making a living, and bringing up a family. The
            matchbox with the odd looking casings was nearly forgotten
            until I had the chance to read a book by L.R. Wallack. His
            book MODERN ACCURACY was published back in 1951, and was
            brought to my attention by Gerhard Steding a member of the
            Pine Tree Rifle Club of Johnstown New York, and long time
            friend.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            Wallack's Book was written at a time when modern benchrest
            shooting was in its infancy. There were no 222 Remingtons,
            or 6mm PPCs on the line at the benchrest shoots. It was a
            time when the 219 Donaldson Wasp was well established, and
            being challenged by a hot wildcat known as the 22-250. There
            were other lesser-known wildcats also being shot in
            benchrest matches, but the race for top dog was between
            these two for the most part.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            If you could go back in time to 1949 and walk the firing
            line at a BR match held at the Pine Tree Rifle Club I'm sure
            that you would find it interesting to say the least. It was
            a time when the shooting icons of yesterday walked the earth
            at the <b>Pine Tree Rifle Club</b>. Your walk back in time
            would have you rubbing elbows with Harvey Donaldson, Warren
            Page, John Unertl, Fred Huntington, Mike Walker, Robert and
            Clyde Hart, Col. Townsend Whelen, Al Barr, and the like. </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            You would find the equipment that they used very primitive
            by today's standers. The Benchrest shooters and their
            equipment were on the frontier of rifle accuracy, as we know
            it today. Photos in Wallack's book show that stock design
            was yet something to be worked out. Some stocks were not
            only massive but looked like it had just come off the rough
            saw at the mill. You would see some of these rifles with
            massive wood stocks being supported by makeshift front
            pedestals, and common sandbags. Photos show front pedestals
            that were made from the swivel base of a discarded office
            chair, and several that were just wooden blocks with
            sandbags piled atop.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            Scopes were much longer than we are accustom to today, they
            had external adjustment rings, and names like Lyman, and
            Unertl. . Many of the rifles in use were built on Mauser
            actions, and some were being shot in their modified military
            stocks. There were Winchester model 70s on the firing line
            straight from the hardware store shelf, and rifles with
            barrels that resembled anti-aircraft artillery. Shooters
            using phrases like �My rifle shoots in the mid-ones, and low
            two's would not be heard. Back then if your rifle shot under
            a inch at 100-yards it was considered very accurate. If it
            shot under a half inch at 100-yards it was something blessed
            by the Gods!
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            It was a time of ideas and development, and one area that
            was receiving great attention by several experimenters was
            cartridge design. This was a new generation of shooters that
            had emerged from a time before them that had seen the advent
            of smokeless powder come to be and the decline of the black
            powder cartridge. The time period between the late 1880s and
            1930s was a time of struggle between cartridge advancement
            into the smokeless powder cartridges and the old norms that
            shooters carried with them about black powder cartridges.
            The shooters of old were not immediately sold on smokeless
            powder cartridges and the smaller bullets at higher rates of
            speed. So arms manufactures had to cater to both schools of
            thought to get a part of the market. It was the dark-ages of
            cartridge development.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            When benchrest shooting took off shortly after WWII the new
            generation of shooters went into competition with what they
            had available. There were a few wildcat varmint-hunting
            cartridges invented before WWII that exhibited exceptional
            accuracy for their time so they were pressed into service
            for the new sport. The BR shooters were soon experimenting
            with new wildcat cartridge designs to achieve better
            accuracy. Thus was born the Golden Age of Wildcats. The list
            of new wildcat cartridges to come along is long, and some of
            the names given the new cartridges were colorful to say the
            least. It became a balancing act for the inventors that had
            endless combinations of bullets, primer, and powders being
            tried in different case designs. There were many that fell
            along the way, and some that became unquestionably
            successful. The 222 Remington, and 6MM PPC are two that
            became standards in modern accuracy.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;
            Some of this renaissance in cartridge design is described in
            Wallack's book, and can be seen in the number of wildcat
            cartridges shown within the text. On page 93 and 94 he makes
            mention of a 6mm Magnum. The following is taken from
            Wallack's book.
          </p>
          <div class="legacy-a8f4a90dad">
            <p>&nbsp;More recently, work has been done by Charlie Morse,
              Joe Hodge, Bill Cotter, and myself on various versions of
              the 6mm. The one which has been seen the most at benchrest
              shoots is a short belted magnum case. The case is 2.2
              inches long, has a 30-degree shoulder, and a neck length
              of 3/8 inch, and uses the standard 300-magnum body taper.</p>
            <p>&nbsp;The best load so far is 43-grains of 4350 and a
              90-grain bullet. Robert Pelzer, of Little Falls, New York,
              used this cartridge on an Enfield action at the 1950
              Johnstown shoot. He averaged .51 inch for the five shot
              100-yard group and .67 inch for the five five-shot and
              five ten-ten shot groups. This is average accuracy for
              this combination.</p>
          </div>
          <p>&nbsp; Reading this, the light went on and I remembered the
            matchbox with the odd looking casing given to me decades
            ago. I dug through the trunk that holds my accumulation of
            old cartridges I have acquired over the past fifty years,
            and found the matchbox. Could it be that the cartridge
            casings I have in the matchbox were the same as those
            described in Wallack's book. I thought that there might be a
            good chance and started taking measurements with my dial
            calipers. It fit like a glove!</p>
          <p>&nbsp;Wallack's shop was located in Mayfield, New York, a
            few miles from my home. The Pine Tree Rifle Club is located
            a few miles in the other direction from my home, so things
            were starting to add up. The previous owner of the home that
            the box of old cartridges came from was not to be found, so
            a search was started to find the location of Wallack's Gun
            Shop.</p>
          <p>&nbsp;Dick Lorey, a member of the Pine Tree Rifle Club
            since the 1950s, and my handgun-shooting mentor back in the
            late 1960s was contacted. I ask Dick if he recalled a
            gunsmith in the Mayfield area during the 1950s by the name
            of Wallack. Dick, now at age 84 years young, with a mind as
            sharp as a stepmothers tong, replied �Sure, he was up on the
            Mountain Road just north of Mayfield�. Dick's lovely wife
            Beverly added to the conversation. I remember the Wallack
            family. I baby-sat their kids. I asked Dick if he would mind
            talking a ride with me and point out the place where the
            Wallack Gun Shop was years ago. </p>
          <p>We traveled north to Mayfield and then on the Mountain
            Road. It was a cold and snowy day that was warmed by
            listening to Dick. Dick is a window to the past for me. I
            enjoy listening to him talk about the old days at the PTRC
            and the early days of benchrest shooting there. Dick worked
            the benchrest shoots scoring the targets back then. Although
            he never shot in the benchrest matches he admired the
            shooters and learned to apply their techniques to his rifles
            and shooting. Dick is most adept at getting the most
            accuracy out of factory rifles with hand loads. </p>
          <p>&nbsp;We had traveled a few miles up the Mountain Road when
            Dick asked me to slow down. There, there it is, he said
            pointing out the passenger side window. The building was now
            a private residence and there was nothing to be seen that
            resembled a gun smith shop. </p>
          <p>&nbsp;The location of Wallack's gun smith Shop was directly
            across the road from the house where I was given the box
            containing the matchbox of odd old casings. At this point it
            was all the proof that I needed to know what I had
            suspected. I have cartridge casings from the 6mm Magnum
            Wallack mentioned in his book, a rare wildcat from the early
            days of modern benchrest shooting. As far as I know I have
            the only 13 casing remaining from that experiment in
            accuracy.</p>
          <p>&nbsp;To most folks they are but a few ounces of dirty
            scrap brass, but to me they are one of the treasures
            directly involved in those early days of modern benchrest
            shooting. </p>
          <p>&nbsp;The accuracy we shooters now enjoy was not
            commonplace sixty short years ago. All of us who enjoy using
            accurate rifles owe a debt of gratitude to those who went
            before us on the frontier of modern accuracy,<i> they were
              the benchrest shooters on the firing line at the Pine Tree
              Rifle Club.</i></p>
          <div class="legacy-a8f4a90dad">
            <p>To those who broke trail, I tip my hat and say Thank you.</p>
            <p class="legacy-fb5ec070f2">May all your shots find their intended
              mark,</p>
            <p class="legacy-37dd0c2a64">John A Del Savio</p>
          </div></div></div></div>


